` the pattern then counts as its own. Where the rule passed, the call gives what it gave and
` goes on where it ended; where not, the call fails. An error the rule raises leaves the
` function with the call. A rule made into several functions, whose names a panic or an error
` would show, is called only where it can do neither. A call that gives the rule a pattern
` whose function may raise errors calls the function made for such a pattern, as
` `emit-raising-rule` says, and an error it answers with leaves the function with the call.
` A call given collectors calls the
` function made for them, lending it the caller's lists, and gives no value, as does one
` `spanned`, which gives the text it matched, but as `spanned-value` says, and calls the function
